// ---------- Abstract ----------
children.push(
  h1(T`Abstract`),
  mix([
    T`Background: Milk tea (typically 35–50 g sugar and 100–200 mg caffeine per serving) has never been prospectively studied for atrial fibrillation (AF) and is not instrumented in genome-wide association studies (GWAS). We tested whether genetically proxied intake of its sweetened-beverage components is causally associated with AF.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Methods and Results: Seven beverage exposures were instrumented in UK Biobank GWAS (N = 64,949–447,485); AF statistics came from Nielsen 2018 (60,620 cases; primary), non-overlapping FinnGen R13 (67,783 cases; co-primary), AFGen 2018 and BioBank Japan (East Asian context). Inverse-variance-weighted estimates were complemented by robust estimators, multivariable MR, colocalization and LD-score regression. No sweetened-beverage exposure showed evidence of a causal association with AF (European meta-analytic odds ratios 0.82–0.97; P ≥ 0.06). A nominal coffee association (OR 1.34, 1.11–1.62; P = 0.002) was not robust: weighted-median estimates were null, outlier robustness was source-dependent, the estimate attenuated in multivariable models with weak conditional instruments (OR 0.82; P = 0.099), and colocalization was confined to the adiposity locus FTO. Sweetened-beverage traits nonetheless showed positive genetic correlations with AF (rg = 0.24–0.31; FDR q < 0.05). Plasma caffeine and paraxanthine instruments were unassociated with AF (P ≥ 0.31). Null genetic estimates converge with randomized trial evidence.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Conclusions: Genetically proxied sweetened-beverage intake showed no evidence of a causal association with AF; moderate-to-large effects are excluded, but small effects are not. The modest observational association likely reflects adiposity-related confounding; direct milk-tea studies in East Asian cohorts remain needed.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Keywords: Mendelian randomization; atrial fibrillation; sugar-sweetened beverages; coffee; genetic correlation; causal inference`,
  ])
);

