// ---------- Discussion ----------
children.push(
  h1(T`Discussion`),
  h2(T`Principal findings`),
  mix([
    T`In this two-sample and multivariable Mendelian randomization study using the largest available European-ancestry AF GWAS, genetically proxied consumption of sweetened beverages showed no evidence of a causal association with atrial fibrillation, and the null estimates exclude moderate-to-large but not small effects. This null finding held across five conceptually distinct exposures (including sugar added to tea or coffee, the closest genetic proxy for milk-tea-type beverages), three European outcome sources and all complementary estimators. Three nominal IVW signals emerged, but none survived scrutiny: the inverse association for sugar added to coffee failed to replicate in the larger primary outcome; a nominal inverse association for sugar added to tea in FinnGen R13 was absent from the other three sources and attenuated in the meta-analysis; and the positive association for coffee intake also failed scrutiny despite nominal significance after Benjamini–Hochberg correction (meta-analytic OR 1.34; P = 0.002). East Asian replication estimates were imprecise but directionally consistent. The positive lifestyle control was directionally associated with AF in all four cohorts and significant in the European meta-analysis, confirming analytical sensitivity.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Comparison with previous work`),
  mix([
    T`Beyond previous single-beverage and dietary-exposure MR analyses [8,29,40,41], the present study contributes a unified multi-exposure framework: five sweetened-beverage phenotypes and their caffeinated counterparts examined against the largest public AF GWAS, with adiposity modeled jointly, locus-level colocalization, and an East Asian contextual arm. Our findings diverge from the two UK Biobank cohort analyses that reported 10–20% higher incident AF risk among high consumers of SSBs or ASBs [2,3]. A previous smaller MR study of SSB and pure-fruit-juice intake reported a positive causal association between SSBs and AF (OR 1.023, 1.007–1.038; P = 0.0039) [29]. That analysis used an AF outcome GWAS with 3,818 cases—one-sixteenth the size of ours—used a relaxed instrument threshold (P < 10⁻⁵) and did not model adiposity; the reported effect lies within our confidence bounds, and our multivariable, colocalization and CAUSE analyses argue against a causal interpretation. Three considerations help reconcile this divergence. First, the observational hazard ratios were modest, compatible with residual confounding: sweetened-beverage consumers differ systematically from non-consumers in adiposity, diet quality and lifestyle, and our multivariable and colocalization results demonstrate directly that adiposity-related pathways account for the only nominal genetic signal we observed. Second, MR estimates reflect lifelong average exposure, whereas cohorts capture mid-life intake over about a decade; if a true effect requires cumulative exposure beyond instrumented intake, MR would be biased toward the null. Third, our confidence bounds exclude causal effects larger than ~9–20% relative increase per intake unit for most exposures, so any small true effect lies below what current instruments can detect.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Instrument specificity and horizontal pleiotropy`),
  mix([
    T`Genetic instruments for self-reported beverage intake are not molecule-specific: they index intake behavior together with its adiposity, dietary-quality and socioeconomic correlates, so horizontal pleiotropy is the expectation rather than the exception for traits of this kind [32,33]. Three features of the design mitigate this concern. First, pleiotropy of this type manufactures false-positive signals, not precise false negatives: the headline estimates are null with confidence bounds that exclude the observational effect sizes, a pattern that would be improbable were a moderate causal effect present. Second, the single nominal signal that did arise was dissected rather than accepted at face value—source-dependent outlier sensitivity, attenuation once BMI was modeled jointly, and colocalization confined to the adiposity locus FTO jointly show the framework detecting and isolating pleiotropy rather than concealing it. Third, all exposures were re-estimated under estimators with orthogonal robustness assumptions (weighted median, contamination mixture, MR-RAPS, CAUSE, MRlap), with concordant results. We nonetheless interpret these estimates as effects of the behavior cluster the instruments index, not of a molecularly isolated exposure, and frame our conclusions accordingly.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`For coffee, the literature is mixed: a smaller MR study reported no causal coffee–AF association [8]; observational and genetic evidence points to no harm [4,31], although healthy-user bias may color inverse estimates [10]. Evidence now converges across three independent designs: susceptibility (genetic nulls here and previously [8,33,44]), acute triggering (day-long randomized exposure showed no atrial arrhythmia excess [30]) and recurrence (39% lower hazard with randomized caffeinated coffee [9]), as recently reviewed [46]. Our nominal IVW association does not overturn that consensus: it was heterogeneous, non-robust to sensitivity estimators, vanished once BMI was modeled jointly, and source-dependent after outlier correction. Colocalization reinforced this reading: the coffee signal had no AF counterpart at the CYP1A1–CYP1A2 and AHR loci (PP.H4 ≤ 0.008) but shared a causal variant with AF at FTO (PP.H4 = 0.894; replicated at 0.906); tea showed the same dissociation (Table S21), and caffeine-metabolism-locus instruments were null while the remainder carried the signal. Consumption-based instruments index intake behavior and its correlates rather than circulating caffeine [32]; plasma-caffeine MR links caffeine itself to lower adiposity and returned null associations with major cardiovascular diseases, AF included [33], reinforcing that the adiposity pathway here reflects beverage-drinking patterning, not caffeine pharmacology. On balance, the genetic evidence does not establish a causal effect of habitual coffee intake on AF in either direction. The 2026 American Heart Association statement left caffeine-metabolism effect modification unresolved [10]; our locus-level analyses speak directly to that gap.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Tea is less studied: one Chinese cohort linked low-dose green tea to lower incident AF [34]; our null tea estimates extend that literature causally and concur with a previous null MR of tea intake against cardiovascular outcomes [41].`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Potential mechanisms and the role of adiposity`),
  mix([
    T`The BMI positive control (OR 1.47 per SD) reaffirms adiposity as a central causal determinant of AF and offers a parsimonious explanation for the observational literature: habitual SSB intake promotes weight gain, and the resulting adiposity, rather than the beverage per se, increases AF risk via atrial remodelling, epicardial fat and hypertension. Two-step mediation MR quantified this pathway: the BMI-mediated indirect effect was similar in magnitude to the nominal coffee association (Table S16). Strong colocalization of the coffee signal with AF at FTO, and its absence at caffeine-metabolism loci, provides locus-level corroboration, and the positive genetic correlations of SSB and ASB intake with AF (rg = 0.24–0.31, q < 0.05) alongside null MR estimates exemplify the distinction: sweetened-drink intake shares heritable architecture with AF without a detectable causal beverage effect. This distinction shifts preventive emphasis toward weight management and dietary pattern [1].`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Clinical and public-health implications`),
  mix([
    T`Most fundamentally, the component-proxy design estimates the marginal, lifelong effects of individual ingredients rather than the effect of the composite beverage: a null for sugar, caffeine and tea does not entail a null for milk tea, which additionally involves possible sugar–caffeine interaction, non-instrumented constituents (non-dairy creamer with its saturated and trans-fatty-acid load; tapioca toppings of high glycaemic index), and an acute per-serving character that a per-category lifelong estimand does not capture. The present data illustrate why component-level inference is fragile: fizzy- and low-calorie-drink intake share genetic architecture with AF (rg = 0.24–0.31) despite null MR estimates, and the nominal coffee signal colocalized with AF only through the adiposity locus FTO. Component-level nulls with pleiotropically entangled architectures therefore cannot certify the safety of a composite beverage they do not instrument; the acute time window and dose–response shape are questions for randomized ambulatory-monitoring designs [30].`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Current genetic evidence does not support sweetened beverage intake as a direct causal determinant of AF. Plasma metabolite instruments extended this inference from behavior to biology: genetically higher caffeine and paraxanthine were unassociated with AF, and cis estimation at CYP2A6 was null, converging with the absent colocalization at caffeine-metabolism loci [44]; relative to the two-variant plasma-caffeine analysis whose null findings included atrial fibrillation [33], it adds independent multi-locus instruments and tests paraxanthine and theobromine. It offers no direct evidence implicating milk-tea-type beverages in AF etiology but cannot exclude effects of observational magnitude. Case reports link high-dose energy drinks to acute AF in young, low-risk individuals [35]. Our null accords with US guideline advice against caffeine avoidance for AF prevention [36]. This is no verdict of safety: the instruments proxy intake categories rather than the acute sugar–caffeine bolus of milk tea, and the case for limiting added sugar and sweeteners rests on evidence far beyond AF [38]. For young East Asian consumers—where global estimates place a rising SSB-attributable cardiovascular burden [45]—the AF-prevention message remains the established one: healthy weight, limited alcohol, cardiometabolic risk control [1]. Prospective studies measuring milk tea intake directly in East Asian cohorts remain a priority.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Strengths and limitations`),
  mix([
    T`Strengths include multiple complementary estimators, the largest public European-ancestry AF GWAS, pre-specified multiplicity control, multivariable modelling and colocalization, a positive lifestyle control and an East Asian contextual arm. Several limitations merit emphasis. First, no GWAS measures milk tea itself: the component-proxy strategy assumes instrumented sweetening behavior approximates the exposure of interest, ordinal phenotypes scale estimates per intake category, and MR indexes lifelong average exposure, so acute triggering, post-ablation recurrence and any J-shaped dose–response lie outside the estimand [10]. Second, sub-phenotype instruments used a relaxed threshold (P < 5 × 10⁻⁶); although all F statistics exceeded 20 (Table S22), F captures neither winner's curse nor correlated pleiotropy, and two sub-phenotypes lack public full summary statistics; the concordant MR-RAPS (Table S23) and MRlap estimates (difference P > 0.05; Table S17) mitigate, but do not eliminate, this concern. Third, instruments derive from European-ancestry adults whereas target consumers are young East Asians; the BioBank Japan analysis is subject to cross-ancestry mismatch, and the early-onset analysis was underpowered [37]. Fourth, diagnostic codes under-detect paroxysmal AF, and the FinnGen I9_AF endpoint pools AF with atrial flutter (ICD-10 I48). Fifth, UK Biobank contributes to both the exposure GWAS and Nielsen 2018; with F > 20 overlap bias should be minimal, and the LDSC intercept for coffee × Nielsen 2018 (−0.011) was null whereas it detected known coffee–tea overlap (−0.318). Sixth, the biomarker arm carried two to seven instruments per analyte, captured steady-state plasma levels from single measurements rather than habitual intake, and suffered incomplete FinnGen coverage of rare metabolite-associated variants (1 of 5 caffeine loci; 2 of 7 paraxanthine loci), so metabolite estimates are supportive rather than definitive. Finally, multivariable estimates rest on conditionally modest instruments (conditional F 1.3–7.5; Table S24), mediation MR was a mechanistic probe under product-method assumptions, and the deferred NHANES triangulation would have been uninformative by design.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`A concern common to all behavioral-exposure instruments—including published alcohol, coffee and smoking MR analyses and our positive control—is that preference-associated variants may act through correlated behavioral, socioeconomic or metabolic pathways, and no sensitivity analysis can establish biological specificity; how much this matters remains methodologically debated [43]. Here it is bounded by cross-estimator concordance, participant-disjoint replication and null metabolite-instrument estimates. Our estimates are bounds on total beverage-component-pathway effects, not proof of zero effect.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Conclusions`),
  mix([
    T`Genetically proxied consumption of sweetened beverages—including the sugar component of tea- and coffee-based drinks, the closest available proxy for milk-tea-type beverages—showed no evidence of a causal association with atrial fibrillation in European or East Asian populations: moderate-to-large effects are excluded, but small effects, and milk-tea exposure itself, remain untested. A positive lifestyle control confirmed that the design detects established causal effects. The modest observational association between sweetened beverages and incident AF is more plausibly explained by residual confounding, including adiposity, than by a direct causal effect of beverage sugar. Prevention efforts for AF are better directed at established cardiometabolic risk factors; direct investigation of milk tea consumption in prospective East Asian cohorts is needed to close the remaining evidence gap.`,
  ])
);

